-- title:
--   Two fraction-field presentations with equal kernels are κ-isomorphic
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be a field, $B$ a commutative ring, and $K_1,K_2$ fields carrying $\kappa$-algebra structures. Let $r_1 : B \to K_1$ and $r_2 : B \to K_2$ be ring homomorphisms such that $\ker r_1 = \ker r_2$, and assume each $K_i$ is generated by $r_i$ as a field of fractions in the strong elementwise sense: for every $x \in K_1$ there are $g,h \in B$ with $r_1(h) \neq 0$ and $x\, r_1(h) = r_1(g)$, and likewise for every $x \in K_2$ there are $g,h \in B$ with $r_2(h) \neq 0$ and $x\, r_2(h) = r_2(g)$. Assume further given an index type $C$ together with maps $c : C \to B$ and $c_\kappa : C \to \kappa$, with $c_\kappa$ surjective, such that $r_1(c(t)) = \mathrm{algebraMap}_{\kappa,K_1}(c_\kappa(t))$ and $r_2(c(t)) = \mathrm{algebraMap}_{\kappa,K_2}(c_\kappa(t))$ for every $t \in C$; thus the two $\kappa$-structures are realised by one and the same family of elements of $B$. The conclusion is the existence of a $\kappa$-algebra isomorphism $e : K_1 \simeq K_2$ with $e(r_1(b)) = r_2(b)$ for all $b \in B$.
--
--   This is the uniqueness of the field of fractions, in the form needed when a field is presented twice over a common ring of "integers": two such presentations with the same kernel agree, compatibly with the presentations and with the field of constants read off through a surjective family of constants in $B$. It is used in the analysis of modular curves at full level to identify a field obtained as fractions of reductions with an abstractly given function field, and is cited by several statements about inertia and Igusa-type separability there.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_RingHom_exists_algEquiv_comp_eq_of_ker_eq_of_forall_exists_mul_eq.lean

